NNAAMMEE
     ppffccttll — control the packet filter (PF) device

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppffccttll [--ddeegghhNNnnPPqqrrvvzz] [--aa _a_n_c_h_o_r] [--DD _m_a_c_r_o=_v_a_l_u_e] [--FF _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r] [--ff _f_i_l_e]
           [--ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e] [--KK _k_e_y] [--kk _k_e_y] [--LL _s_t_a_t_e_f_i_l_e] [--oo _l_e_v_e_l]
           [--pp _d_e_v_i_c_e] [--SS _s_t_a_t_e_f_i_l_e] [--ss _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r [--RR _i_d]]
           [--tt _t_a_b_l_e --TT _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s _._._.]] [--VV _r_d_o_m_a_i_n] [--xx _l_e_v_e_l]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppffccttll utility communicates with the packet filter device using the
     ioctl interface described in pf(4).  It allows ruleset and parameter con‐
     figuration, and retrieval of status information from the packet filter.
     Packet filtering restricts the types of packets that pass through network
     interfaces entering or leaving the host based on filter rules as de‐
     scribed in pf.conf(5).  The packet filter can also replace addresses and
     ports of packets.

     The packet filter is enabled by default.  Should ppffccttll be unable to load
     a ruleset, an error occurs and the original ruleset remains in place.  If
     this happens at system startup, the ruleset defined by the _R_U_L_E_S variable
     in rc(8) remains in place.

     The packet filter does not itself forward packets between interfaces.
     Forwarding can be enabled by setting the sysctl(8) variables
     _n_e_t_._i_n_e_t_._i_p_._f_o_r_w_a_r_d_i_n_g and/or _n_e_t_._i_n_e_t_6_._i_p_6_._f_o_r_w_a_r_d_i_n_g to 1.  Set them
     permanently in sysctl.conf(5).

     At least one option must be specified.  The options are as follows:

     --aa _a_n_c_h_o_r
             Apply flags --ff, --FF, --ss, --TT, and --zz only to the rules in the spec‐
             ified _a_n_c_h_o_r.  In addition to the main ruleset, ppffccttll can load
             and manipulate additional rulesets by name, called anchors.  The
             main ruleset is the default anchor.

             Anchors are referenced by name and may be nested, with the vari‐
             ous components of the anchor path separated by ‘/’ characters,
             similar to how file system hierarchies are laid out.  The last
             component of the anchor path is where ruleset operations are per‐
             formed.

             Evaluation of _a_n_c_h_o_r rules from the main ruleset is described in
             pf.conf(5).

             For example, the following will show all filter rules (see the --ss
             flag below) inside the anchor “authpf/smith(1234)”, which would
             have been created for user “smith” by authpf(8), PID 1234:

                   # pfctl -a "authpf/smith(1234)" -s rules

             Private tables can also be put inside anchors, either by having
             table statements in the pf.conf(5) file that is loaded in the an‐
             chor, or by using regular table commands, as in:

                   # pfctl -a foo/bar -t mytable -T add 1.2.3.4 5.6.7.8

             When a rule referring to a table is loaded in an anchor, the rule
             will use the private table if one is defined, and then fall back
             to the table defined in the main ruleset, if there is one.  This
             is similar to C rules for variable scope.  It is possible to cre‐
             ate distinct tables with the same name in the global ruleset and
             in an anchor, but this is often bad design and a warning will be
             issued in that case.

             By default, recursive inline printing of anchors applies only to
             unnamed anchors specified inline in the ruleset.  If the anchor
             name is terminated with a ‘*’ character, the --ss flag will recur‐
             sively print all anchors in a brace delimited block.  For example
             the following will print the “authpf” ruleset recursively:

                   # pfctl -a 'authpf/*' -sr

             To print the main ruleset recursively, specify only ‘*’ as the
             anchor name:

                   # pfctl -a '*' -sr

             To flush all rulesets and tables recursively, specify only ‘*’ as
             the anchor name:

                   # pfctl -a '*' -Fa

     --DD _m_a_c_r_o=_v_a_l_u_e
             Define _m_a_c_r_o to be set to _v_a_l_u_e on the command line.  Overrides
             the definition of _m_a_c_r_o in the ruleset.

     --dd      Disable the packet filter.

     --ee      Enable the packet filter.

     --FF _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r
             Flush the filter parameters specified by _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r (may be abbre‐
             viated):

             rruulleess      Flush the filter rules.
             ssttaatteess     Flush the state table (NAT and filter).
             SSoouurrcceess    Flush the source tracking table.
             iinnffoo       Flush the filter information (statistics that are not
                        bound to rules).
             TTaabblleess     Flush the tables.
             oossffpp       Flush the passive operating system fingerprints.
             RReesseett      Reset limits, timeouts and other options back to de‐
                        fault settings.  See the OPTIONS section in pf.conf(5)
                        for details.
             aallll        Flush all of the above.

             If --aa is specified as well and _a_n_c_h_o_r is terminated with a ‘*’
             character, rruulleess, TTaabblleess and aallll flush the given anchor recur‐
             sively.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Replace the current ruleset with the rules contained in _f_i_l_e.
             This _f_i_l_e may contain macros, tables, options, and normalization,
             queueing, translation, and filtering rules.  With the exception
             of macros and tables, the statements must appear in that order.

     --gg      Include output helpful for debugging.

     --hh      Help.

     --ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
             Restrict the operation to the given _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e.

     --KK _k_e_y  Kill all of the source tracking entries originating from the host
             or network specified by _k_e_y.  A second --KK option may be speci‐
             fied, which will kill all the source tracking entries from the
             first host/network to the second.

     --kk _k_e_y  Kill all of the state entries originating from the host or net‐
             work specified by _k_e_y.  A second --kk option may be specified,
             which will kill all the state entries from the first host/network
             to the second.

             A network prefix length of 0 can be used as a wildcard.  To kill
             all states with the target “host2”:

                   # pfctl -k 0.0.0.0/0 -k host2

             It is also possible to kill states by rule label, state key, or
             state ID.  In this mode the first --kk argument is used to specify
             the type; a second --kk gives the actual target.

             To kill states by rule label, use the llaabbeell modifier.  To kill
             all states created from rules carrying the label “foobar”:

                   # pfctl -k label -k foobar

             To kill one specific state by its state key (as shown by pfctl -s
             state), use the kkeeyy modifier.  To kill a state originating from
             10.0.0.101:32123 to 10.0.0.1:80, protocol TCP, use:

                   # pfctl -k key -k 'tcp 10.0.0.1:80 <- 10.0.0.101:32123'

             To kill one specific state by its unique state ID (as shown by
             pfctl -s state -vv), use the iidd modifier.  To kill a state with
             ID 4823e84500000003 use:

                   # pfctl -k id -k 4823e84500000003

             To kill a state with ID 4823e84500000018 created from a backup
             firewall with hostid 00000002 use:

                   # pfctl -k id -k 4823e84500000018/2

     --LL _s_t_a_t_e_f_i_l_e
             Load pf states from the file specified by _s_t_a_t_e_f_i_l_e.

     --NN      Do not perform domain name resolution.  If a name cannot be re‐
             solved without DNS, an error will be reported.

     --nn      Do not actually load rules, just parse them.

     --oo _l_e_v_e_l
             Control the ruleset optimizer, overriding any rule file settings.

             nnoonnee       Disable the ruleset optimizer.
             bbaassiicc      Enable basic ruleset optimizations.  This is the de‐
                        fault behaviour.
             pprrooffiillee    Enable basic ruleset optimizations with profiling.

             For further information on the ruleset optimizer, see pf.conf(5).

     --PP      Print ports using their names in _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s if available.

     --pp _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Use the device file _d_e_v_i_c_e instead of the default _/_d_e_v_/_p_f.

     --qq      Only print errors and warnings.

     --rr      Perform reverse DNS lookups on states and tables when displaying
             them.  --NN and --rr are mutually exclusive.

     --SS _s_t_a_t_e_f_i_l_e
             Store the pf state table in the file specified by _s_t_a_t_e_f_i_l_e.

     --ss _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r [--RR _i_d]
             Show the filter parameters specified by _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r (may be abbrevi‐
             ated):

             qquueeuuee        Show the currently loaded queue definitions.  When
                          used together with --vv, per-queue statistics are also
                          shown.  When used together with --vv --vv, ppffccttll will
                          loop and show updated queue statistics every five
                          seconds, including measured bandwidth and packets
                          per second.
             rruulleess        Show the currently loaded filter rules.  If --RR _i_d is
                          specified as well, only the rule with the specified
                          numeric ID is shown.  When used together with --vv,
                          the per-rule statistics (number of evaluations,
                          packets and bytes) are also shown.  When used to‐
                          gether with --gg or --vvvv, expired rules (marked as “#
                          expired”) are also shown.  Note that the “skip step”
                          optimization done automatically by the kernel will
                          skip evaluation of rules where possible.  Packets
                          passed statefully are counted in the rule that cre‐
                          ated the state (even though the rule isn't evaluated
                          more than once for the entire connection).
             AAnncchhoorrss      Show the currently loaded anchors directly attached
                          to the main ruleset.  If --aa _a_n_c_h_o_r is specified as
                          well, the anchors loaded directly below the given
                          _a_n_c_h_o_r are shown instead.  If --vv is specified, all
                          anchors attached under the target anchor will be
                          displayed recursively.
             ssttaatteess       Show the contents of the state table.  If --RR _i_d is
                          specified as well, only states created by the rule
                          with the specified numeric ID are shown.
             SSoouurrcceess      Show the contents of the source tracking table.
             iinnffoo         Show filter information (statistics and counters).
                          When used together with --vv, source tracking statis‐
                          tics, the firewall's 32-bit hostid number and the
                          main ruleset's MD5 checksum for use with pfsync(4)
                          are also shown.
             llaabbeellss       Show per-rule statistics (label, evaluations, pack‐
                          ets total, bytes total, packets in, bytes in, pack‐
                          ets out, bytes out, state creations) of filter rules
                          with labels, useful for accounting.  If --RR _i_d is
                          specified as well, only the statistics for the rule
                          with the specified numeric ID are shown.
             ttiimmeeoouuttss     Show the current global timeouts.
             mmeemmoorryy       Show the current pool memory hard limits.
             TTaabblleess       Show the list of tables.
             oossffpp         Show the list of operating system fingerprints.
             IInntteerrffaacceess   Show the list of interfaces and interface groups
                          available to PF.  When used together with --vv, it ad‐
                          ditionally lists which interfaces have skip rules
                          activated.  When used together with --vvvv, interface
                          statistics are also shown.  --ii can be used to select
                          an interface or a group of interfaces.
             aallll          Show all of the above, except for the lists of in‐
                          terfaces and operating system fingerprints.

             Counters shown with --ss iinnffoo are:

             match           explicit rule match
             bad-offset      currently unused
             fragment        invalid fragments dropped
             short           short packets dropped
             normalize       dropped by normalizer: illegal packets
             memory          memory could not be allocated
             bad-timestamp   bad TCP timestamp; RFC 1323
             congestion      network interface queue congested
             ip-option       bad IP/IPv6 options
             proto-cksum     invalid protocol checksum
             state-mismatch  packet was associated with a state entry, but se‐
                             quence numbers did not match
             state-insert    state insertion failure
             state-limit     configured state limit was reached
             src-limit       source node/connection limit
             synproxy        dropped by synproxy
             translate       no free ports in translation port range
             no-route        dropped by no-route

     --tt _t_a_b_l_e --TT _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s _._._.]
             Specify the _c_o_m_m_a_n_d (may be abbreviated) to apply to _t_a_b_l_e.  Com‐
             mands include:

             aadddd            Add one or more addresses to a table.  Automati‐
                            cally create a persistent table if it does not ex‐
                            ist.
             ddeelleettee         Delete one or more addresses from a table.
             eexxppiirree _n_u_m_b_e_r  Delete addresses which had their statistics
                            cleared more than _n_u_m_b_e_r seconds ago.  For entries
                            which have never had their statistics cleared,
                            _n_u_m_b_e_r refers to the time they were added to the
                            table.
             fflluusshh          Flush all addresses in a table.
             kkiillll           Kill a table.
             rreeppllaaccee        Replace the addresses of the table.  Automatically
                            create a persistent table if it does not exist.
             sshhooww           Show the content (addresses) of a table.
             tteesstt           Test if the given addresses match a table.
             zzeerroo           Clear all the statistics of a table, or only for
                            specified addresses.

             For the aadddd, ddeelleettee, rreeppllaaccee, and tteesstt commands, the list of ad‐
             dresses can be specified either directly on the command line
             and/or in an unformatted text file, using the --ff flag.  Comments
             starting with a ‘#’ are allowed in the text file.  With these
             commands, the --vv flag can also be used once or twice, in which
             case ppffccttll will print the detailed result of the operation for
             each individual address, prefixed by one of the following let‐
             ters:

             A    The address/network has been added.
             C    The address/network has been changed (negated).
             D    The address/network has been deleted.
             M    The address matches (tteesstt operation only).
             X    The address/network is duplicated and therefore ignored.
             Y    The address/network cannot be added/deleted due to conflict‐
                  ing ‘!’ attributes.
             Z    The address/network has been cleared (statistics).

             Each table can maintain a set of counters that can be retrieved
             using the --vv flag of ppffccttll.  For example, the following commands
             define a wide open firewall which will keep track of packets go‐
             ing to or coming from the OpenBSD FTP server.  The following com‐
             mands configure the firewall and send 10 pings to the FTP server:

                   # printf "table <test> counters { ftp.openbsd.org }\n \
                       pass out to <test>\n" | pfctl -f-
                   # ping -qc10 ftp.openbsd.org

             We can now use the table sshhooww command to output, for each address
             and packet direction, the number of packets and bytes that are
             being passed, matched or blocked by rules referencing the table.
             Note that the match counters are incremented for every match rule
             in which they are referenced, meaning that a single packet may be
             counted multiple times.  The time at which the current accounting
             started is also shown with the “Cleared” line.

                   # pfctl -t test -vTshow
                      198.51.100.81
                           Cleared:        Fri Jun 28 11:17:37 2013
                           In/Block:       [ Packets: 0    Bytes: 0        ]
                           In/Match        [ Packets: 54   Bytes: 10028    ]
                           In/Pass:        [ Packets: 5    Bytes: 1949     ]
                           Out/Block:      [ Packets: 0    Bytes: 0        ]
                           Out/Match       [ Packets: 65   Bytes: 12684    ]
                           Out/Pass:       [ Packets: 6    Bytes: 389      ]

             Similarly, it is possible to view global information about the
             tables by using the --vv modifier twice and the --ss TTaabblleess command.
             This will display the number of addresses on each table, the num‐
             ber of rules which reference the table, and the global packet
             statistics for the whole table:

                   # pfctl -vvsTables
                   --a-r-C test
                           Addresses:   1
                           Cleared:     Fri Jun 28 11:17:37 2013
                           References:  [ Anchors: 0       Rules: 4        ]
                           Evaluations: [ NoMatch: 35      Match: 8        ]
                           In/Block:    [ Packets: 0       Bytes: 0        ]
                           In/Match:    [ Packets: 54      Bytes: 10028    ]
                           In/Pass:     [ Packets: 5       Bytes: 1949     ]
                           In/XPass:    [ Packets: 0       Bytes: 0        ]
                           Out/Block:   [ Packets: 0       Bytes: 0        ]
                           Out/Match:   [ Packets: 65      Bytes: 12684    ]
                           Out/Pass:    [ Packets: 6       Bytes: 389      ]
                           Out/XPass:   [ Packets: 0       Bytes: 0        ]

             Only packets creating state are matched in the Evaluations line,
             but all packets passing as a result of the state are correctly
             accounted for.  Reloading the table(s) or ruleset will not affect
             packet accounting in any way.  The two “XPass” counters are in‐
             cremented instead of the “Pass” counters when a “stateful” packet
             is passed but doesn't match the table anymore.  This will happen
             in our example if someone flushes the table while the ping(8)
             command is running.

             When used with a single --vv, ppffccttll will only display the first
             line containing the table flags and name.  The flags are defined
             as follows:

             c    For constant tables, which cannot be altered outside
                  pf.conf(5).
             p    For persistent tables, which don't get automatically killed
                  when no rules refer to them.
             a    For tables which are part of the _a_c_t_i_v_e tableset.  Tables
                  without this flag do not really exist, cannot contain ad‐
                  dresses, and are only listed if the --gg flag is given.
             i    For tables which are part of the _i_n_a_c_t_i_v_e tableset.  This
                  flag can only be witnessed briefly during the loading of
                  pf.conf(5).
             r    For tables which are referenced (used) by rules.
             h    This flag is set when a table in the main ruleset is hidden
                  by one or more tables of the same name from anchors attached
                  below it.
             C    This flag is set when per-address counters are enabled on
                  the table.

     --VV _r_d_o_m_a_i_n
             Select the routing domain to be used to kill states by host or by
             label.  The rdomain of a state is displayed in parentheses before
             the host by --ss ssttaatteess.

     --vv      Produce more verbose output.  A second use of --vv will produce
             even more verbose output including ruleset warnings.  See the
             previous section for its effect on table commands.

     --xx _l_e_v_e_l
             Set the debug _l_e_v_e_l, which limits the severity of log messages
             printed by pf(4).  This should be a keyword from the following
             ordered list (highest to lowest): eemmeerrgg, aalleerrtt, ccrriitt, eerrrr,
             wwaarrnniinngg, nnoottiiccee, iinnffoo, and ddeebbuugg.  These keywords correspond to
             the similar (LOG_) values specified to the syslog(3) library rou‐
             tine, and may be abbreviated on the command line.

     --zz      Clear per-rule statistics.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/pf.conf  Packet filter rules file.
     /etc/pf.os    Passive operating system fingerprint database.

